# Frontend Basic Rules

## Core Principles
- Prefer simple, readable components over clever abstractions.
- Keep one responsibility per component; split when logic grows.
- Use clear naming for state, handlers, and props (`isLoading`, `handleSubmit`).

## React Conventions
- Use function components and hooks.
- Keep side effects in `useEffect`; avoid side effects during render.
- Derive values from state/props instead of duplicating state.
- Use early return for loading, error, and empty states.

## State and Data
- Use local state first; introduce global state only when sharing is necessary.
- For async data, always model `loading`, `error`, and `success`.
- Never mutate objects/arrays in state directly; use immutable updates.

## API and Error Handling
- Wrap async calls in `try/catch`.
- Show user-friendly error messages; log raw errors for debugging.
- Guard optional responses (example: `error.response?.data`).

## UI and Accessibility
- Use semantic HTML (`button`, `label`, `main`, `nav`).
- Every form input must have an accessible label.
- Ensure keyboard interaction works for clickable elements.

## Quality Harness
- Before finishing work, run `npm run build` and resolve failures.
- Fix lint errors in files you changed.
- Add or update small test coverage for critical logic changes.

## Example Generation Policy
- When the user asks for examples, always provide 10 or more examples.
- Every example must include source code (not concept-only bullets).
- Prefer varied cases (basic, error handling, edge case, practical usage).

## Example Pattern
```jsx
async function submitForm(payload) {
  try {
    setLoading(true);
    setError('');
    const data = await api.save(payload);
    setResult(data);
  } catch (e) {
    console.error(e);
    setError(e.message || '요청 처리 중 오류가 발생했습니다.');
  } finally {
    setLoading(false);
  }
}
```
